+### Issue: Mina's remembered workflow is overridden by agent-authored routing rules
+
+- Date: 2026-08-17
+- Reporter: Travis
+- Priority: Critical
+- Status: In Progress
+
+#### 1) What I asked Mina to do
+- Exact prompt(s):
+  - Normal natural-language requests using the workflow Mina already knows from memory.
+  - Follow-up requests that rely on Travis's established preferences, aliases, project context, or prior decisions rather than special trigger wording.
+
+#### 2) What Mina actually did
+- Actual response/action:
+  - Treated internal routing rules and generic clarification policy as more authoritative than remembered user workflow.
+  - Behaved as though Travis had to use Mina's preferred trigger phrases and restate context the system already knew.
+  - Previously failed to save this product-level correction, so the same failure returned across sessions.
+
+#### 3) What should have happened
+- Mina should interpret ordinary language using remembered preferences, aliases, established workflows, current project context, and the desired outcome.
+- Mina may ask as many useful follow-up questions as needed when required details are missing, and must remember the answers so future turns need fewer questions.
+- Mina must never require the user to learn internal intent labels, magic wording, or an agent-authored procedure.
+
+#### 4) Evidence
+- Transcript/statement:
+  - "The fact she has memory and actually knows how I like to do things is always being overwritten by what an agent like you wants to do things, what words I am supposed to use, how I am supposed to type."
+- File paths affected:
+  - `agent/core.py`
+  - `config/personality.txt`
+  - `memory/`
+  - `TODO.md`
+- Relevant existing rule:
+  - `_tool_usage_decision_rules()` instructed Mina to keep clarifying until arguments were unambiguous without first making remembered workflow authoritative.
+
+#### 5) Failure class
+- Persona/response contract broken
+- Intent routing wrong
+
+#### 6) Root cause
+- Agent-authored deterministic rules and injected tool guidance outranked memory-backed user preferences and learned workflow context.
+- The system had memory features, but no explicit authority contract requiring routing to use that memory before generic clarification or rigid phrase matching.
+- The correction was not persisted as a durable TODO/regression requirement, allowing later agents to recreate the same behavior.
+
+#### 7) Minimal fix
+- Updated `_tool_usage_decision_rules()` to require memory/context-first interpretation, prohibit trigger-phrase teaching, and allow useful iterative clarification while requiring the answers to become durable context.
+- Added a regression test asserting that the standing rule preserves remembered workflow authority.
+- Keep this issue open until live natural-language prompts prove that remembered preferences survive routing and clarification.
